ou’re absolutely right that the burial process isn’t perfectly gentle—it can collapse roofs and bend walls. **But that collapse is part of the preservation mechanism, not its negation.** And the “crumbling ruins” you see at a site like Troy or Knossos are often a mixture of 3000-year-old destruction, later stone robbing, excavation damage, and modern weathering—not simply the result of layers slowly pressing down.
